{
  "id": "p12",
  "title": "Sorting and Searching",
  "lessons": [
    {
      "id": "p12-l1",
      "title": "Bubble Sort",
      "xp": 15,
      "intro": [
        "Bubble sort walks through the array again and again and swaps every pair of neighbors that are in the wrong order. The big values bubble to the end.",
        "After the first pass, the biggest value is in the last place; after the second, the second biggest is in place, and so on. So each pass can stop one place earlier.",
        "It's slow for big lists, but short and easy to get right. Perfect for a high score table."
      ],
      "example": {
        "code": [
          "program Bubble;",
          "type",
          "  TList = array[1..6] of integer;",
          "const",
          "  nums: TList = (64, 25, 12, 22, 11, 90);",
          "var",
          "  a: TList;",
          "  i: integer;",
          "",
          "procedure Sort(var a: TList; n: integer);",
          "var",
          "  i, j, t: integer;",
          "begin",
          "  for i := 1 to n - 1 do",
          "    for j := 1 to n - i do",
          "      if a[j] > a[j + 1] then",
          "      begin",
          "        t := a[j];",
          "        a[j] := a[j + 1];",
          "        a[j + 1] := t;",
          "      end;",
          "end;",
          "",
          "begin",
          "  for i := 1 to 6 do",
          "    a[i] := nums[i];",
          "  Sort(a, 6);",
          "  for i := 1 to 6 do",
          "    write(a[i], ' ');",
          "  writeln;",
          "end."
        ],
        "output": [
          "11 12 22 25 64 90 "
        ]
      },
      "exercises": [
        {
          "id": "p12-l1-e1",
          "type": "multiple_choice",
          "prompt": "What does one pass of bubble sort do?",
          "options": [
            "Swaps neighbors that are in the wrong order, all the way through",
            "Finds the smallest value",
            "Sorts the whole array",
            "Removes duplicates"
          ],
          "answer": 0,
          "explanation": "After one pass, the biggest value is at the end."
        },
        {
          "id": "p12-l1-e2",
          "type": "multiple_choice",
          "prompt": "Where is the biggest value after the first pass?",
          "options": [
            "At the end",
            "At the start",
            "In the middle",
            "Anywhere"
          ],
          "answer": 0,
          "explanation": "It bubbles all the way up."
        },
        {
          "id": "p12-l1-e3",
          "type": "fill_blank",
          "prompt": "Swap when the left neighbor is bigger.",
          "code": [
            "    if a[j] ___ a[j + 1] then"
          ],
          "options": [
            ">",
            "<",
            "=",
            "<>"
          ],
          "answer": ">",
          "explanation": "For small to big, a bigger left value is in the wrong place."
        },
        {
          "id": "p12-l1-e4",
          "type": "multiple_choice",
          "prompt": "How do you sort from big to small instead?",
          "options": [
            "Swap when a[j] < a[j + 1]",
            "Run the loop backwards",
            "Use two arrays",
            "It's not possible"
          ],
          "answer": 0,
          "explanation": "Just flip the comparison."
        },
        {
          "id": "p12-l1-e5",
          "type": "order_lines",
          "prompt": "Put the swap in the right order.",
          "lines": [
            "t := a[j];",
            "a[j] := a[j + 1];",
            "a[j + 1] := t;"
          ],
          "answer": [
            "t := a[j];",
            "a[j] := a[j + 1];",
            "a[j + 1] := t;"
          ],
          "explanation": "Save one value first, or it's lost."
        },
        {
          "id": "p12-l1-e6",
          "type": "multiple_choice",
          "prompt": "Why can each pass stop one place earlier?",
          "options": [
            "The end of the array is already sorted",
            "The array gets shorter",
            "To save memory",
            "It can't"
          ],
          "answer": 0,
          "explanation": "After pass i, the last i values are in place."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "p12-l2",
      "title": "Linear Search",
      "xp": 15,
      "intro": [
        "To find a value, look at one element after the other until you find it, or reach the end. That's linear search.",
        "A function can return the index where it found the value, or 0 if it's not there.",
        "A while loop fits well, because it can stop as soon as the value is found."
      ],
      "example": {
        "code": [
          "program Find;",
          "type",
          "  TNames = array[1..4] of string;",
          "const",
          "  langs: TNames = ('FORTRAN', 'BASIC', 'Pascal', 'C');",
          "",
          "function Find(var a: TNames; n: integer; name: string): integer;",
          "var",
          "  i: integer;",
          "begin",
          "  i := 1;",
          "  while (i <= n) and (a[i] <> name) do",
          "    i := i + 1;",
          "  if i <= n then",
          "    Find := i",
          "  else",
          "    Find := 0;",
          "end;",
          "",
          "begin",
          "  writeln('Pascal is at ', Find(langs, 4, 'Pascal'));",
          "  writeln('Java is at ', Find(langs, 4, 'Java'));",
          "end."
        ],
        "output": [
          "Pascal is at 3",
          "Java is at 0"
        ]
      },
      "exercises": [
        {
          "id": "p12-l2-e1",
          "type": "multiple_choice",
          "prompt": "How does linear search work?",
          "options": [
            "It checks one element after the other",
            "It jumps to the middle",
            "It sorts first",
            "It guesses"
          ],
          "answer": 0,
          "explanation": "Simple, and works on unsorted arrays."
        },
        {
          "id": "p12-l2-e2",
          "type": "multiple_choice",
          "prompt": "What should a search function return if the value isn't there?",
